                <h3>Chapter 1: The Meeting</h3>
                <p>Alex walked into the atelier wearing motorcycle boots and carrying a toolbox. "I brought my own tools," she said, dead serious. Then she smiled. "Just kidding. But I do want my dress to feel like it could survive a motorcycle ride."</p>
                
                <p>She showed me her inspiration board — equal parts Victorian machinery and dark fairy tales. Gears next to wings. Industrial blueprints beside enchanted forests. "I want to look dangerous but beautiful," she said. "Like I could fix an engine and then disappear into the mist."</p>
                
                <p>That contradiction — industrial strength with ethereal beauty — became our design challenge.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="card">
                <h3>Chapter 2: The Sketch</h3>
                <p>I spent a week in my workshop surrounded by gears, tulle, and metal scraps. The breakthrough came when I stopped trying to blend the two aesthetics and started letting them conflict.</p>
                
                <p>What if the structure was aggressively angular but the skirt was pure fantasy? What if delicate tulle wrapped around functional gear details? The tension between them would create the magic.</p>
                
                <p>When Alex saw Design C, she didn't cry. She pumped her fist. "YES. This is the rebellion I wanted."</p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="card">
                <h3>Chapter 3: The Materials Hunt</h3>
                <p>Finding the right charcoal silk faille took three weeks and samples from five countries. The Japanese mill had exactly what we needed — structured but with movement. Not rigid, but rebellious.</p>
                
                <p>The tulle was easier. We found a French mill that made this incredible charcoal tulle with just enough sheen, and an Italian supplier for the plum undertones. Layering them creates depth you can't look away from.</p>
                
                <p>The gears came from a watchmaker in Germany who thought I was insane until I explained the concept. Then he got excited and started suggesting modifications.</p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="card">
                <h3>Chapter 4: The First Fitting</h3>
                <p>Even in plain muslin, the silhouette stopped everyone in the studio. Alex stood differently in it — taller, more powerful. She kept looking at her reflection from different angles.</p>
                
                <p>"The shoulder," she said, touching the angular structure. "It makes me feel like a warrior."</p>
                
                <p>When I mentioned pockets, her face lit up. "Pockets! In a wedding dress! You understand me."</p>
                
                <p>She left the atelier walking like she owned the sidewalk outside.</p>
            </div>
            
